# Branch Integration Protocol

Repo: KumonScan (Node/Express + Postgres backend via Neon, React/Vite/MUI frontend).
Multi-tenant: every table is scoped by `center_id`; requests resolve `req.center`
via slug (`/api/c/:slug/...`) or default (`/api/...`). All feature routers are
aggregated in `server/routes/index.js` and mounted twice in `server/app.js`
(slugScoped + defaultScoped) — never mount a new router directly in `app.js`.

We integrate a backlog of pre-existing feature branches (built by separate
agents in parallel worktrees, each unaware of the others) onto `main` via
`git merge --no-commit --no-ff <branch>`, one at a time. Pattern so far,
without exception: every branch was written against an OLDER version of
`main` and/or without knowledge of the multi-tenant retrofit, so integration
has found a REAL bug in every single merge — usually a query with no
`center_id` filter (cross-tenant data leak: one center's staff could read or
trigger actions for another center's data). Assume this will happen again.
After resolving mechanical merge conflicts, explicitly audit every new or
changed query in the branch for a missing `center_id` / `req.center.id`
filter before considering the merge done.

Also check: does the branch add a new table with a FK to `students(id)` or
`sessions(id)` with no `ON DELETE CASCADE`? If so, add it to
`wipeCenterData`'s explicit child-table cleanup in `server/tests/helpers.js`
(dependency order: grandchildren before children before students), or the
shared test suite will break for every other test file that shares the Neon
test branch.

## Verification workflow for each merge

1. `git merge --no-commit --no-ff <branch>`
2. Resolve conflicts. Read both sides fully before choosing — usually both
   sides' logic needs to survive (branches predate features `main` already has).
3. Audit new/changed queries for `center_id` scoping (see above).
4. `cd client && npm run build` — must pass clean.
5. `cd server && npx vitest run` — run the FULL suite (not just the new
   branch's test file), since a shared Neon test-branch means one file's
   bug can cascade into unrelated files. A single "socket hang up" or
   "fetch failed" `NeonDbError` is a transient network blip — re-run just
   that file in isolation to confirm before treating it as real. Any other
   failure is real; fix it, don't ignore it.
6. Watch for stale test assumptions: a branch's own test suite may assert
   "when feature X doesn't exist" (e.g., "no delivery channel installed")
   — if X now exists on `main` (because a sibling branch already merged
   it), that assumption is false post-merge and the test needs rewriting
   to match real integrated behavior, not the code.
7. `git add` each changed file EXPLICITLY by name (not `-A`). Double check
   with `git status` after adding that everything shows fully staged (no
   trailing `M` after an `A` — that means an edit didn't get staged; this
   has bitten this repo once already). If any `git add` command errors on
   a bad pathspec, the WHOLE command may silently fail to stage anything —
   verify with `git status` immediately after every `git add`.
8. Commit with a message explaining what conflicts were resolved and how,
   and any real bugs found/fixed during integration (not just "merged X").
9. Update `.agent-coordination/status.md` (gitignored, not committed): mark
   the branch's row "merged to main (commit `<hash>`)".

Stripe billing (`agent-billing`) is explicitly excluded from all
integration — do not merge it, do not reference it as pending.
